Overall winner: Within-subjects designs offer a methodological alternative to between-groups approaches by comparing each participant to themselves across conditions rather than comparing one group of participants to another. In between-groups designs, individual differences between participants introduce nuisance variability (“noise”) that can obscure true effects. Because each participant is measured in only one condition, relatively large samples are needed—for example, 40 people to obtain 20 data points per condition. In contrast, within-subjects designs collect data from the same individuals under all conditions, reducing participant-related variability and requiring fewer total participants (e.g., just 20 people to yield 20 data points per condition). This approach produces more powerful statistical tests with greater efficiency.

Several specific forms of within-subjects designs were introduced. Pretest–posttest designs expose each participant to both a pretest and posttest surrounding an intervention. Repeated-measures designs involve two or more systematically varied conditions within a session, while longitudinal designs measure the same individuals at multiple time points over months or years. Each type takes advantage of comparing participants to themselves to detect change or differences more sensitively.

Despite these benefits, within-subjects designs face certain challenges. Participants may guess a study’s purpose (demand characteristics) or experience carryover effects, where performance in one condition influences later ones. Carryover effects include fatigue, practice, history, maturation, and testing effects. Each can distort results if the order of conditions coincides with these changes. Slides illustrating problematic examples (“What’s wrong with this picture?”) emphasized the importance of diagnosing order effects by comparing outcomes across different condition sequences.

Researchers combat order effects using counterbalancing—varying or randomizing condition order across participants. Complete counterbalancing methods, such as ABBA and block randomization, include all possible condition orders; however, the number of orders grows quickly with added conditions. Incomplete counterbalancing methods like random rotation or Latin-square designs provide practical solutions by ensuring each condition appears equally often in each position and follows every other condition equally often. In cognitive science, conditions are sometimes intermixed randomly within many trials to minimize order bias, unless doing so would confuse participants or overly lengthen the task. Nonetheless, counterbalancing has practical limits: irreversible manipulations, such as drug administration, learning experiences, or surgeries, cannot be undone. Overall, within-subjects designs efficiently reduce noise and increase statistical power but require careful attention to order and carryover control.  

(Word count: 398)
Overall winner: Within-subjects designs offer an efficient alternative to between-groups designs by comparing each participant’s performance across multiple conditions rather than comparing separate groups. In between-groups designs, individual differences add nuisance variability (“noise”) that can obscure true effects, requiring larger samples to reach adequate statistical power—for example, 40 participants to obtain 20 data points per condition. In within-subjects designs, each participant provides data in every condition, comparison is made within the same person, and much of this participant-related noise is removed. With 20 participants tested under all conditions, researchers can still achieve 20 usable data points per condition, producing a more powerful and efficient test.

Three main forms of within-subjects designs were emphasized. **Pretest–posttest designs** measure participants before and after an intervention. **Repeated-measures designs** include two or more manipulated conditions within a session. **Longitudinal designs** involve repeated measurements across long time spans, yielding highly informative data but requiring substantial time investment.

While these designs reduce noise, they also introduce unique challenges. Participants who experience all phases of a study may discern its purpose (**demand characteristics**) or show **carryover effects**, where one condition influences later performance—either improving it (**practice effects**) or worsening it (**fatigue effects**). In addition, other threats may co-occur but are conceptually distinct from order: **history effects** (external events during the study), **maturation effects** (developmental or biological change), and **testing effects** (performance shifts from repeated exposure to the same test). Each can confound interpretation if not properly controlled.

**Order effects** highlight why condition sequence matters—performance may appear better simply because a condition was run first or second. To manage this, researchers use **counterbalancing**, varying the order of conditions across participants. **Complete counterbalancing** includes every possible order (e.g., ABBA for two conditions or all six orders for three conditions), but the number of orders increases factorially (n!), which becomes impractical with more conditions. **Incomplete counterbalancing** methods, such as random rotation or **Latin-square designs**, ensure balanced exposure while requiring fewer participants. In cognitive science, researchers sometimes randomly intermix trials instead of counterbalancing when many short items are available, though this is avoided when tasks are lengthy or confusing. Some manipulations cannot be counterbalanced at all—participants cannot “un-take” a drug, “unlearn” a skill, or repeat a one-time experience.  

